Subject: AOT: total coverage, generator/async resume and the refusal census

What this does
  Turns the P2 skeleton into a step-only unit that covers every function the
  register backend compiles, including generators and async functions, and
  reports why anything was left out.

  Binding and eligibility no longer require JS_FUNC_NORMAL: a generator or
  async function whose bytecode is register-compiled is bound like any other.
  When js_regvm_execute reaches a bound function it enters the generated body
  with the interpreter's resume marker, and on every generator/async exit
  (return, yield, await, uncaught throw) the generated body hands control back
  through done_generator exactly as the interpreter does.

  The unit generator now walks every function bytecode, not only the eligible
  ones, and classifies each: eligible, still-lazy, refused by the register
  backend, empty, or too big (AOT_GEN_MAX_LEN = 1<<18 register bytes). That
  census is what qjsaot prints; nothing in the box2d or test262 corpus is
  refused, so refused profiled ops are 0.008% on box2d (a single top-level
  <eval>).

  qjs_aot_unwind() also gains an AOT_MUT_SKIP_UNWIND hook: with that macro the
  exception walk is skipped, which is the negative control the kernel gate
  requires (a kernel must then fail, not silently pass).

Why we need it
  P2 could only run straight-line leaf functions; the whole point of the phase
  is that generators, async/await and exceptions survive the boundary. The
  census is the evidence that coverage is total rather than merely sufficient
  for the sample corpora.

What it adds
  quickjs.c
      js_aot_bind()/aot_gen_eligible() accept every register-compiled
      function; the AOT entry in js_regvm_execute carries the resume marker in
      and routes every generator/async exit to done_generator; js_aot_gen_collect()
      collects and classifies all function bytecodes; qjs_aot_unwind() gains the
      AOT_MUT_SKIP_UNWIND hook.
  quickjs-aot-gen.h
      The AOT_GEN_REF_* reason enum, AOT_GEN_MAX_LEN and the eligible/stack_len
      fields the census reports.

  Nothing here is compiled without -DQJS_REGVM; QJS_AOT is off by default.
  Box2D runs fully compiled with checksum 546.1008391043414, test262-fast stays
  at 50/81192, and every kernel checksum matches the interpreter.

